---
slug: api/support/inbound/sendgrid(/:token)
method: post
format: json
layout: ''
---
{%- comment -%}
  The same endpoint as `support/inbound/sendgrid`, under the path that the
  one Inbound Parse host on this SendGrid account that actually delivers
  uses: `/api/support/inbound/sendgrid/<secret>`.

  Ours has never received a post. Every other variable has been matched or
  ruled out, so this route exists to take the path out of the comparison: a
  second Parse host can point here and differ from the working one in
  nothing but its hostname and its instance.

  A path prefix should not matter. The original route answers POSTs from
  the public internet and logs them, which is the only thing a prefix could
  affect. This is here to prove that rather than assume it. If a post
  arrives on one route and not the other, the prefix is load bearing and
  that is a finding worth having.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  comment
    Named keys only, never the params hash itself. See the partial.
  endcomment
  function result = 'modules/support/helpers/email/sendgrid_post', email: context.params.email, envelope: context.params.envelope, token: context.params.token

  assign status = result.status | default: 200
  if status != 200
    response_status status
  endif

  assign body = { "ok": result.ok, "error": result.error, "ticket_number": result.ticket_number, "new_ticket": result.new_ticket, "reopened": result.reopened }
  echo body | json
-%}
